WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.022
A convex function lies at or below every chord joining two points on its graph

00:00:06.022 --> 00:00:14.267
This is a statement about all mixtures of two inputs, not simply about a graph looking bowl shaped in a particular drawing.

00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:18.032
Take inputs negative one and three

00:00:18.032 --> 00:00:22.140
Their midpoint is one, where the square function equals one

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.837
The midpoint of their two output values is five

00:00:25.837 --> 00:00:30.767
The lower function value illustrates the convexity inequality for this particular pair.

00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:37.875
A convex feasible set contains the whole segment between any two of its points

00:00:37.875 --> 00:00:39.950
An interval has this property

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:47.833
Keeping mixtures feasible is crucial when using a convex objective to compare a nearby candidate with a distant alternative.

00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:54.643
For a convex objective on a convex set, every local minimum is global

00:00:54.643 --> 00:01:02.271
If a lower point existed, convexity would give lower values along the segment arbitrarily near the supposed minimum

00:01:02.271 --> 00:01:06.933
That contradiction explains the conclusion; a plotted example alone would not.
